---
title: "Tutorial 3: A phylogenomic workflow with Snakemake"
description: Run SEGUL, IQ-TREE, and ASTRAL as one reproducible Snakemake workflow, from 4,040 UCE alignments to concatenated and species trees.
sidebar:
  order: 3
  label: "3. Workflow with Snakemake"
---

This tutorial builds a workflow that takes a published set of alignments to two phylogenetic trees with one command. SEGUL does the alignment work, and other software infers the trees. You will:

1. Install Snakemake.
2. Describe the software of each step.
3. Write the workflow, one rule at a time.
4. Run it and check the results.
5. Change a setting and rerun only the steps it affects.

## The software

| Software | Role in the workflow |
| --- | --- |
| [Snakemake](https://snakemake.github.io/) | Runs the steps in order, in parallel where it can, and only when their input changed. |
| [SEGUL](/docs/cli-usage/intro/) | Summarizes, filters, trims, and concatenates the alignments. |
| [IQ-TREE](https://iqtree.github.io/) | Infers a maximum likelihood tree from the supermatrix, and one gene tree per locus. |
| [ASTRAL](https://github.com/chaoszhang/ASTER) | Estimates a species tree from the gene trees. |

You install only Snakemake. It installs the other three from [Bioconda](https://bioconda.github.io/) the first time the workflow runs.

## Before you start

- **A computer with Linux or macOS.** On Windows, use [WSL](https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/install).
- **Conda 24.7.1 or newer.** [Miniforge](https://conda-forge.org/download/) is a good way to get it. Check with `conda --version`.
- **About 1 GB of free storage**, and an internet connection for the first run.
- **Basic use of a terminal.** You do not need to know Snakemake or Python.

With 8 cores, the whole workflow takes about 10 minutes.

## The dataset

The data are the same shrew UCE alignments as in [Tutorial 1](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-1/), from [Esselstyn et al. 2021](https://doi.org/10.1206/0003-0090.454.1.1): 4,040 NEXUS alignments of 102 taxa, in a 14.6 MB archive on [Zenodo](https://zenodo.org/records/6459213). The workflow downloads it.

## The workflow

| Step | Rule | Software | Output |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Download and unpack the alignments | `download`, `unpack` | curl, unzip | `data/loci` |
| Summarize the raw alignments | `summary_raw` | SEGUL | `results/01-summary-raw` |
| Keep loci with enough taxa | `filter` | SEGUL | `results/02-filtered` |
| Trim sites with missing data | `trim` | SEGUL | `results/03-trimmed` |
| Summarize the clean alignments | `summary_clean` | SEGUL | `results/04-summary-clean` |
| Concatenate the loci | `concat` | SEGUL | `results/05-supermatrix` |
| Infer the concatenated tree | `concat_tree` | IQ-TREE | `results/06-concat-tree` |
| Infer a tree per locus | `gene_tree` | IQ-TREE | `results/07-gene-trees` |
| Estimate the species tree | `collect_gene_trees`, `species_tree` | ASTRAL | `results/08-species-tree` |

## 1. Install Snakemake

Create a conda environment with Snakemake and activate it:

```bash
conda create --name snakemake --channel conda-forge --channel bioconda snakemake-minimal conda
```

```bash
conda activate snakemake
```

Check the installation. This tutorial was tested with Snakemake 9.27:

```bash
snakemake --version
```

## 2. Create the project

A Snakemake project is a folder with a `Snakefile`, a configuration file, and one file per software environment:

```plaintext
segul-phylo/
├── Snakefile
├── config.yaml
└── envs/
    ├── segul.yaml
    ├── iqtree.yaml
    └── aster.yaml
```

```bash
mkdir -p segul-phylo/envs
```

```bash
cd segul-phylo
```

Create the files in the next sections with any text editor.

## 3. Describe the software

Each file in `envs` lists the conda channels and one package with its version. Fixed versions give the same results on another computer or a year from now.

```yaml title="envs/segul.yaml"
channels:
  - conda-forge
  - bioconda
dependencies:
  - segul=0.25.0
```

```yaml title="envs/iqtree.yaml"
channels:
  - conda-forge
  - bioconda
dependencies:
  - iqtree=3.1.4
```

The `aster` package contains ASTRAL:

```yaml title="envs/aster.yaml"
channels:
  - conda-forge
  - bioconda
dependencies:
  - aster=1.25
```

## 4. Set the options

Settings you may want to change go in `config.yaml`, so you never edit the commands themselves:

```yaml title="config.yaml"
# Alignments to download: a zip archive of NEXUS files, one per locus.
dataset_url: "https://zenodo.org/records/6459213/files/esselstyn_et_al_2021_uce_aln.zip?download=1"

# Name of the supermatrix and the trees.
prefix: shrews

# Keep loci with at least this proportion of the taxa.
min_taxa: 0.75

# Remove sites where more than this proportion of the taxa is missing.
max_missing: 0.5

# Substitution model for IQ-TREE.
model: GTR+G

# Extra IQ-TREE options. `--fast` runs a quick tree search, which suits a
# first look. Set it to "" for a full search.
iqtree_options: "--fast"
```

## 5. Write the Snakefile

A Snakefile is a list of rules. A rule names its `input` files, its `output` files, and the `shell` command that makes one from the other. Snakemake reads the rules backward from the files you ask for and works out which commands to run.

Add each block below to the `Snakefile`, in order.

### Settings and targets

The first lines load the settings. The rule `all` lists the final files of the workflow: the two summaries and the two trees.

```python title="Snakefile"
configfile: "config.yaml"

PREFIX = config["prefix"]
TRIMMED = "results/03-trimmed/trimmed_alignments"


rule all:
    input:
        "results/01-summary-raw/raw_alignment_summary.txt",
        "results/04-summary-clean/clean_alignment_summary.txt",
        f"results/06-concat-tree/{PREFIX}.treefile",
        f"results/08-species-tree/{PREFIX}.tre",
```

### Get the data

```python title="Snakefile"
rule download:
    output:
        "data/alignments.zip",
    params:
        url=config["dataset_url"],
    shell:
        "curl --location --silent --show-error --output {output} '{params.url}'"


rule unpack:
    input:
        "data/alignments.zip",
    output:
        directory("data/loci"),
    shell:
        "unzip -q -j {input} '*.nex' -d {output}"
```

`directory()` marks an output that is a folder. `unzip -j` leaves out the folder inside the archive, so the alignments land in `data/loci`.

### Summarize and filter with SEGUL

```python title="Snakefile"
rule summary_raw:
    input:
        "data/loci",
    output:
        "results/01-summary-raw/raw_alignment_summary.txt",
        "results/01-summary-raw/raw_locus_summary.csv",
        "results/01-summary-raw/raw_taxon_summary.csv",
    log:
        "logs/summary-raw.log",
    conda:
        "envs/segul.yaml"
    shell:
        "segul align summary --dir {input} --input-format nexus "
        "--output results/01-summary-raw --prefix raw --force --log {log}"


rule filter:
    input:
        "data/loci",
    output:
        directory("results/02-filtered"),
    params:
        min_taxa=config["min_taxa"],
    log:
        "logs/filter.log",
    conda:
        "envs/segul.yaml"
    shell:
        # SEGUL adds the threshold to the folder name, such as `_75p`.
        "segul align filter --dir {input} --input-format nexus "
        "--percent {params.min_taxa} --output {output} --log {log} && "
        "mv {output}_*p {output}"
```

Four things make SEGUL fit a workflow:

- **`conda:`** names the environment file, so the rule runs with that SEGUL version.
- **`--log {log}`** sends SEGUL's log file to the `logs` folder. Without it, every step writes `segul.log` in the project folder.
- **`--force`** lets SEGUL write into a folder that already exists. Snakemake creates the folder of a rule's output files before it runs the command.
- **`params:`** passes a setting from `config.yaml` to the command, here `--percent {params.min_taxa}`.

`segul align filter` adds the threshold to the name of its output folder, such as `02-filtered_75p`. The `mv` command gives it back the name that the rule declares.

### Trim, summarize again, and concatenate

```python title="Snakefile"
checkpoint trim:
    input:
        "results/02-filtered",
    output:
        directory("results/03-trimmed"),
    params:
        max_missing=config["max_missing"],
    log:
        "logs/trim.log",
    conda:
        "envs/segul.yaml"
    shell:
        "segul align trim --dir {input} --input-format nexus "
        "--missing-data {params.max_missing} --output {output} --log {log}"


def trimmed_dir(wildcards):
    """The trimmed alignments, once the trim checkpoint has run."""
    checkpoints.trim.get()
    return TRIMMED


rule summary_clean:
    input:
        trimmed_dir,
    output:
        "results/04-summary-clean/clean_alignment_summary.txt",
        "results/04-summary-clean/clean_locus_summary.csv",
        "results/04-summary-clean/clean_taxon_summary.csv",
    log:
        "logs/summary-clean.log",
    conda:
        "envs/segul.yaml"
    shell:
        "segul align summary --dir {input} --input-format nexus "
        "--output results/04-summary-clean --prefix clean --force --log {log}"


rule concat:
    input:
        trimmed_dir,
    output:
        alignment=f"results/05-supermatrix/{PREFIX}.phy",
        partition=f"results/05-supermatrix/{PREFIX}_partition.txt",
    params:
        prefix=PREFIX,
    log:
        "logs/concat.log",
    conda:
        "envs/segul.yaml"
    shell:
        "segul align concat --dir {input} --input-format nexus "
        "--output-format phylip --partition-format raxml "
        "--output results/05-supermatrix --prefix {params.prefix} --force --log {log}"
```

`trim` is a `checkpoint`, not a `rule`. Snakemake cannot know which loci pass the filter before it runs, so it cannot plan one gene tree per locus yet. A checkpoint tells Snakemake to look at the files again after this step and extend its plan.

`segul align trim` writes the alignments to a `trimmed_alignments` folder inside its output folder, next to `trimming_summary.csv`. The function `trimmed_dir` waits for the checkpoint, then gives that folder to the rules that need it.

### Infer the concatenated tree

```python title="Snakefile"
rule concat_tree:
    input:
        f"results/05-supermatrix/{PREFIX}.phy",
    output:
        f"results/06-concat-tree/{PREFIX}.treefile",
    params:
        prefix=f"results/06-concat-tree/{PREFIX}",
        model=config["model"],
        extra=config["iqtree_options"],
    threads: 8
    conda:
        "envs/iqtree.yaml"
    shell:
        "iqtree -s {input} -m {params.model} {params.extra} -T {threads} "
        "--prefix {params.prefix} --redo --quiet"
```

`threads: 8` lets IQ-TREE use up to 8 cores. `--redo` lets IQ-TREE overwrite the files of an earlier run. Without it, IQ-TREE stops when Snakemake reruns the rule.

### Infer gene trees and the species tree

```python title="Snakefile"
rule gene_tree:
    input:
        TRIMMED + "/{locus}.nex",
    output:
        "results/07-gene-trees/{locus}.treefile",
    params:
        prefix="results/07-gene-trees/{locus}",
        model=config["model"],
        extra=config["iqtree_options"],
    conda:
        "envs/iqtree.yaml"
    shell:
        "iqtree -s {input} -m {params.model} {params.extra} -T 1 "
        "--prefix {params.prefix} --redo --quiet"


def gene_trees(wildcards):
    """One gene tree per alignment that the trim checkpoint wrote."""
    checkpoints.trim.get()
    loci = glob_wildcards(TRIMMED + "/{locus}.nex").locus
    return expand("results/07-gene-trees/{locus}.treefile", locus=loci)


rule collect_gene_trees:
    input:
        gene_trees,
    output:
        "results/08-species-tree/gene-trees.tre",
    run:
        with open(output[0], "w") as out:
            for tree in input:
                with open(tree) as file:
                    out.write(file.read())


rule species_tree:
    input:
        "results/08-species-tree/gene-trees.tre",
    output:
        f"results/08-species-tree/{PREFIX}.tre",
    threads: 8
    log:
        "logs/species-tree.log",
    conda:
        "envs/aster.yaml"
    shell:
        "astral4 -t {threads} -i {input} -o {output} 2> {log}"
```

`gene_tree` has a wildcard, `{locus}`. It is one rule that Snakemake runs once per locus, several at a time. The function `gene_trees` lists the trimmed alignments after the checkpoint and asks for one tree for each. `collect_gene_trees` joins the trees into one file, the input that ASTRAL needs.

## 6. Check the plan

A dry run prints what Snakemake would do without running it:

```bash
snakemake --dry-run --cores 8
```

The job table at the end should list 11 jobs:

```plaintext
job                   count
------------------  -------
download                  1
unpack                    1
filter                    1
summary_raw               1
trim                      1
collect_gene_trees        1
concat                    1
summary_clean             1
concat_tree               1
species_tree              1
all                       1
total                    11
```

The gene trees are missing, because Snakemake adds them after the `trim` checkpoint. An error here usually means a typo or a wrong indent in the `Snakefile`. Snakemake names the line.

## 7. Run the workflow

```bash
snakemake --cores 8 --sdm conda
```

- `--cores 8` is the number of cores Snakemake may use. Jobs run side by side up to this limit.
- `--sdm conda` creates the environments in `envs` and runs each rule in its own. This takes a few minutes on the first run only.

After the `trim` checkpoint, the plan grows to 994 steps: one gene tree for each of the 983 loci that passed the filter. The run ends with:

```plaintext
994 of 994 steps (100%) done
```

{% aside type="tip" title="When a step fails" %}
Snakemake prints the rule and its command. SEGUL's own messages are in the `logs` folder. Fix the cause and run the same command again: Snakemake continues from the failed step and keeps the finished ones.
{% /aside %}

## 8. Check the results

```plaintext
results/
├── 01-summary-raw/      raw_alignment_summary.txt, locus and taxon tables
├── 02-filtered/         983 alignments
├── 03-trimmed/          trimmed_alignments/, trimming_summary.csv
├── 04-summary-clean/    clean_alignment_summary.txt, locus and taxon tables
├── 05-supermatrix/      shrews.phy, shrews_partition.txt
├── 06-concat-tree/      shrews.treefile
├── 07-gene-trees/       983 gene trees
└── 08-species-tree/     gene-trees.tre, shrews.tre
```

Compare the two summaries:

```bash
head -n 7 results/01-summary-raw/raw_alignment_summary.txt results/04-summary-clean/clean_alignment_summary.txt
```

| | Raw | Clean |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Taxa | 102 | 102 |
| Loci | 4,040 | 983 |
| Sites | 5,398,947 | 756,449 |
| Missing data | 49.43% | 13.55% |

These match [Tutorial 1](/docs/tutorials/tutorial-1/#what-you-did), which runs the same steps in SEGUI. To browse a summary in the terminal, run `segul view summary` on its `report.json` file (such as `raw_report.json` or `clean_report.json`). See [viewing summaries](/docs/cli-usage/view-summary/).

The two trees are in Newick format, each with 102 taxa:

- `results/06-concat-tree/shrews.treefile`: the maximum likelihood tree of the supermatrix.
- `results/08-species-tree/shrews.tre`: the ASTRAL species tree, with support values on its branches.

Open them in a tree viewer such as [FigTree](https://github.com/rambaut/figtree/releases) or [iTOL](https://itol.embl.de/).

## 9. Change a setting and rerun

Snakemake records how each file was made. Change a setting, and it reruns only the steps that depend on it.

{% steps %}

1. In `config.yaml`, change `min_taxa` to `0.8`.
2. Check the plan:

   ```bash
   snakemake --dry-run --cores 8 --sdm conda
   ```

   Snakemake lists `filter` and every step after it. It leaves out `download`, `unpack`, and `summary_raw`, whose results still hold.

3. Run the workflow again:

   ```bash
   snakemake --cores 8 --sdm conda
   ```

{% /steps %}

The stricter filter keeps 308 loci, so this run is faster.

## Where to go next

{% aside type="caution" title="A first look, not a final analysis" %}
The settings favor speed. `--fast` shortens the tree search, one model is used for all loci, and the concatenated tree has no support values. For a study, set `iqtree_options` to `""` or to the options you need, such as `-B 1000` for ultrafast bootstrap, and choose thresholds from your own summaries.
{% /aside %}

- **Use your own data.** Point `dataset_url` to your archive, or put your alignments in `data/loci` and remove the `download` and `unpack` rules. Change `--input-format` if they are not NEXUS.
- **Add a step.** A new rule needs only its input, output, and command. Every SEGUL command works the same way. See the [CLI guides](/docs/cli-usage/intro/).
- **Use a partitioned model.** `concat` already writes `shrews_partition.txt`. Add it to the `concat_tree` input and pass it to IQ-TREE with `-p`.
- **Run on a cluster.** Snakemake can submit each job to a scheduler such as Slurm. See the [Snakemake documentation](https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/).
- **Share the workflow.** The project folder is all someone needs to repeat the analysis. Keep it under version control, without `data`, `results`, and `.snakemake`.
